<h2>WHAT ARE METEORS?</h2>
<p>Meteors or "shooting stars" as many people call them, come from comets.</p>
<p>Most of the annual meteor showers we observe take place as Earth passes through trails of debris left behind  by active comets orbiting the Sun, casting off little bits of dusty debris in their long tails.</p>

<p>The Perseid meteors come from a comet called Swift-Tuttle, which orbits the Sun every 133 years.</p>
<p>They took their name from the constellation of Perseus, as they appear to radiate from a point high in the north, called the "radiant in the constellation of Perseus.</p>

<p>To observe the shower, you need only point yourself generally toward the north and look up.</p>
